[4] Tournament Analysis on relation channels
  vertices considered/challenged:
    runners, exact fractions, C27 shells, folds, balanced collisions,
    divisor blockers, Kpq incidence, raw energy, and proof obligations.
  chosen vertices: typed relation channels.
  pair observable: role score
    (theorem scale, branch separation, sign visibility, multiplicand
     clearance, old-repo maturity, scalar-decoy resistance)
  switch/gauge: lexicographically larger role score wins.
  fingerprint: score_hist={0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1, 6: 1, 7: 1} c3=0 scc=(1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1) hp=1
  first Hamiltonian path: q/Farey branch > C27 typed shell > multiplicand clearance > Kpq incidence > visible folds > hidden balanced shells > raw sumset/energy > raw runner vertices
  old-repo-maturity-only gauge would flip 5 pairs.

[5] Proof readout
  The old summand/multiplicand graph does not replace the S130-S133
  Farey split.  It refines it.
  Refined proof interface:
    (1) keep exact q/Farey branch as theorem scale;
    (2) inside p=2, use C27 typed shell + multiplicand clearance;
    (3) inside p>=3, use Kpq/K33 incidence + owner packets;
    (4) inside relation-rich residuals, separate visible folds from
        hidden balanced shells before applying additive-energy bounds.
  Proposed next lemma:
    every remaining non-AP/GW q=14 atom either has a C27 typed-shell
    defect handled by petal/lift rigidity, or has a three-owner K33
    incidence packet with enough sign-visible relation mass to feed the
    HYP-2908 tournament-state lift.
